m is  working to complete this package of  innovations we are constantly surveying  the landscape of what is commercially  available and as new things become  commercially available adding them into  the package where they make sense  comparing their performance in the field  to look at whether they're sufficiently  rugged and and robust but we also need  to think about the challenge of  distribution so the phototherapy lights  that I mentioned earlier the MTTs lights  are distributed primarily in Vietnam the  Dro of phototherapy lights are  distributed primarily in India and if  you try and order either set of lights  in Africa you're going to pay more in  shipping than you will to pay for the  cost of the lights so the challenge of  distribution in Africa is really  significant for medical technologies we  also simultaneously need to think about  how do we aggregate demand in the public  sector and the private sector because  it's very difficult to solve the  distribution challenge if you haven't  and one of the things that it will take  to aggregate demand and unlock demand  unlock resources in the public sector  especially is good-quality evidence that  this package of technologies can save  lives and then it can do so in a way  that is cost effective we need to really  demonstrate that we can achieve that  dollar 48 number not just estimate that  we can achieve that number and so I want  to walk you through briefly each of  those elements of what our team is doing  together so let me start with where we  are in terms of innovation this is a  picture that was taken at Lagos  University Teaching Hospital so it's one  of the largest teaching hospitals in  Nigeria and you can see that they are  very well equipped with photo therapy  lights so those are the the D Rev lights  that you see being used at Luth but they  don't have a way to measure bilirubin  concentration and so our team came back  and began developing a point-of-care  tool to measure bilirubin and it  