s and so our team began to  try and develop technologies to address  these needs so for example if you look  at the problem of jaundice there are a  lot of photo therapy lights that are out  there on the market that have been  designed to work in low resource  settings MTTs makes the firefly de rêve  makes brilliance so there's not a neat  little sparrows makes the Billy Hutt  there's not a need we thought for  another appropriate set of photo therapy  lights but there was no commercially  available Billy a Reuben watt serum  bilirubin monitor that we could identify  and so that's a problem that we took on  and began developing and so we've been  working for a number of years to bring  that together and what we did this last  year was we put together the whole set  of 17 technologies and it was really  exciting to see what they all look like  together so this is what we think a  district hospital that has 5000  deliveries per year should be equipped  with so you can see the photo therapy  lights delivery photo therapy here's an  oxygen concentrator and a flow splitter  here you see the CPAP setup with the  concentrator a syringe pump a suction  machine a pulse oximeter  so in incising this for a hospital that  has 5000 deliveries per year  we think that this equipment should have  a lifetime of five years and then it's  really important to minimize the cost of  consumables and if you just work out the  math for how much it costs to provide  this package of technology divided by  the number of deliveries that would be  delivered at a hospital over that five  year period we estimate that this set of  technologies could be delivered for a  dollar 48 per delivery and so I think  the exciting thing is that technology  has the potential to be as affordable as  vaccines which we have been very  effective at scaling up and and this  includes rolled into this as the cost of  distribution as well as the cost of  supplying consumables over that  five-year period so our our team is  working to complete this package of  innovations we are constantly surveying  the landscape of what is commercially  available and as new things become  commercially available adding them into  the package where they make sense  comparing their performance in the field  to look at whether they're sufficiently  rugged and and robust but we also need  to think about the challenge of  distribution so the phototherapy lights  that I mentioned earlier the MTTs lights  are distributed primarily in Vietnam the  Dro of phototherapy lights are  distributed primarily in India and if  you try and order either set of lights  in Africa you're going to pay more in  shipping than you will to pay for the  cost of the lights so the challenge of  distribution in Africa is really  significant for medical technologies we  also simultaneously need to think about  how do we aggregate demand in the public  sector and the private sector because  it's very difficult to solve the  distribution challenge if you haven't  and one of the things that it will take  to aggregate demand and unlock demand  unlock resources in the public sector  especially is good-quality evidence that  this package of technologies can save  lives and then it can do so in a way  that is cost effective we need to really  demonstrate that we can achieve that  dollar 48 number not just estimate that  we can achieve that number and so I want  to walk you through briefly each of  those elements of what our team is doing  together so let me start with where we  are in terms of innovation this is a  picture that was taken at Lagos  University Teaching Hospital so it's one  of the largest teaching hospitals in  Nigeria and you can see that they are  very well equipped with photo therapy  lights so those are the the D Rev lights  that you see being used at Luth but they  don't have a way to measure bilirubin  concentration and so our team came back  and began developing a point-of-care  tool to measure bilirubin and it  consists of a little piece of paper and  plastic there is a membrane here that  you apply a drop of blood from a heel  stick to and then you remove a piece of  paper that exposes adhesive fold it shut  and plasma will flow down to the distal  tip of the device you can see that it  appears white for blood that has a  normal bilirubin level and this is blood  from a baby with an elevated bilirubin  level you can see by eye that it's  yellow you simply insert this little  disposable into the reader that you see  here press the button will shine light  at three different wavelengths through  that piece of paper and report the  bilirubin concentration so we've just  completed and published a pilot study  looking at a clinical assessment of this  compared to a laboratory standard and  you can see here's the bland Altman plot  so we're within plus or minus two  milligrams per deciliter for our  agreement we're very close to  the clea standard with this device and  if we compare that to the performance of  a transcutaneous billy Reuben ometer you  can see this is not from the same  population this was a study done at Luth  and in Nigeria looking at the accuracy  of a transcutaneous billy ray manometer  and you can see there there within minus  2 to plus almost 5 milligrams per  deciliter it's not accurate enough for  that population and if you look at what  would happen to the dollar 48 number if  we replace our ability Reuben ometer  with this device the dollar 48 number  goes over $12 and that increase is  almost exclusive exclusively associated  with the consumables that are needed to  calibrate the transcutaneous bilirubin  ometer so it's sold under a razor razor  blade model which just doesn't work in a  setting where cost is such a big  pressure  similarly delivering fluid to Babies IV  medications or or saline or glucose  solution has to be delivered at  carefully controlled rates and syringe  pumps are a real challenge to keep going  in this environment so we have developed  a very low-cost syringe pump that has a  streamlined user interface that is less  likely to lead to user error because of  the way the the drivetrain is designed  it can run for over 70 hours on a single  battery charge so if the power goes out  as is common in these settings it will  keep delivering fluid this meets IEC  standards for performance for syringe  pumps in the United States and this is  just looking at the accuracy we've  evaluated it both in newborns as well as  in pregnant women who are who have  preeclampsia and are being treated with  magnesium sulfate and in both  populations it meets IEC standards for  performance and then finally I think  this is my favorite technology because  one of the things that  surprised me so much was the invisible  what I think is a medical emergency of  hypothermia there are so many babies who  are dying because they are too cold in  this setting nurses have access to  thermometers but in a setting where  there may be one nurse every 20 babies  it's really difficult for them to have  time to measure temperatures frequently  enough and then they don't necessarily  have access to warmers for babies that  are not stable enough to be receiving  kangaroo mother care so we've designed a  really simple wearable that displays the  temperature and so you can see this baby  was at 35 degrees C that's hypothermic  so there's a blue light on the top they  can easily see just by looking across  the ward if a baby is too cold just  right or has a fever and then intervene  appropriately we use the same sensor to  control a warming crib if the baby does  need to receive additional warming to to  get back to a normal temperature and  this device is completely reusable we  can make it for less than $25 and it's a  really simple technology we've just  completed a pilot study evaluating this  technology and it performed equivalently  to the GE vital signs monitor so how do  we keep things out of the equipment  graveyard all of these technologies are  designed to be rugged but they still  need preventive maintenance and they  still need in some cases reparative  maintenance to address that challenge  we've partnered with local engineering  schools and one of the things that I  think is an amazing opportunity is a lot  of universities in sub-saharan Africa  now are starting biomedical engineering  diploma and degree programs and this is  something that the Ministry of Health  and the Ministry of Education in many  locations are collaborating to do when  you look at  engineering education in this part of  the world it tends to be very  theoretical and students have very few  opportunities to actually do design or  to interact hands-on with equipment in  laboratories and so one of the things  that I'm most excited about is we've  been able to partner with the University  of Malawi and build an engineering  design studio there and so here you see  students who are working in that  engineering design studio they are  developing locally appropriate ways to  repair oxygen concentrators and to  develop spare parts that can be used to  regenerate the compressors and to  regenerate the SIP beds without having  to import expensive replacements and  we're just getting ready to expand this  to another school in Malawi and also the  Darussalam Institute of Technology and I  think that's really what can lead to the  next generation of innovators that can  solve health problems in other parts of  the hospital we've been doing a  bi-directional student exchange now for  the last three years with students from  Malawi coming to Houston students from  rice going to Malawi and participating  in teams to to take on these design  challenges and doing the same thing at  the faculty level we're really excited  to begin to expand that here we go so I  want to talk about the challenge of  distribution because this surprised our  whole team when we really began to  understand how big this challenge was  and what it would take to solve it so as  the poem on a CPAP was developed we  partnered with third stone design and  industrial design firm the very first  generation of the pull money that was  evaluated clinically looked like the  device you see there on the left so it  was in a more rugged enclosure than the  device in the plastic shoebox but it  didn't look like the final product which  has gone  the the CEM our process and so these  devices are now made in the Bay Area by  a manufacturing group called pride  industries they're made a relatively low  volume so over a thousand seep apps have  been sold they've been sold in 30  countries where they're they're being  used and the the CEO of third stone  design has opened up the financials of  the company so they license the  technology from rice and they licensed  it at zero percent royalty and they  guaranteed that they would set their  price within a certain fraction of their  cost of making it and that they would  prioritize filling orders and gobby  eligible countries and so for us and  licensing the technology access was  really the thing that we were most  interested in insuring and so if we look  at the cost to distribute a single  punani device it cost them about five  hundred and fifty dollars to make it  they sell it for eight hundred dollars  and so that leaves you two hundred and  fifty dollars but two hundred fifty  dollars it turns out is not enough to  distribute bomani you've got to ship it  you've got to clear customs you've got  to train users and you've got to provide  service and all of those costs and their  experience add up to seven hundred and  fifty dollars and so every time third  stone ships up Amani out of their  facility basically they're sticking a  five hundred dollar bill on top of it so  I'm not a business person but I know  that this is not a sustainable way to  move forward and that's where I think we  get really excited about the opportunity  to bring together a package of  technologies so just like a one  technology newborn unit can't solve the  problem of improving newborn survival a  one product company cannot be  sustainable without elevating prices to  the point that are not affordable so if  you think about what happens when we're  distributing  package of technologies the cost of  goods is higher we estimate that it will  cost $26,000 to equip that NICU that I  showed you earlier so you've still got  some margin left but the costs of  distribution are not 17 times what they  are for one technology because you can  package them together you can train  people to use them together a service  technician who's making a call for one  piece of equipment can look at the  entire package and so you gain enormous  ability to sustain this in distributing  a package of technologies together and  so our colleagues at third stone are now  working to establish two distribution  hubs and country we're targeting Nigeria  and Tanzania as the initial locations  for those distribution hubs and our team  is focused on targeting a network of  seven countries in Africa that have  partnered with the World Health  Organization and UNICEF to form what's  called the quality equity dignity  Network these countries have committed  to cutting newborn deaths in half within  four years and these seven countries  together represent 46% of births on the  continent and so our hope is that in  targeting these seven countries that  account for almost half the births on  the continent we can get to a tipping  point that will support their their  dissemination much more broadly  throughout the continent we've done  projections for what we think it will  take to make this distribution  organization sustainable and we think it  will take philanthropic investment so  the little white bars here represent  philanthropic investment sales revenues  and then costs and we think within a six  year period it can get to sustainability  and it's based on the actual data of  distributing the the pramanya CPAP  so one of the things that we're really  excited about is the opportunity to  begin to partner with social impact  investors to think about how to target  not just facilities that are public  facilities but to think about how to  target private hospitals in a country  like Malawi almost all of the healthcare  is provided by either government  hospitals or the the faith-based mission  hospitals and so although they're  private it's not the same as a place  like Nigeria for example where more than  half of care in the country is provided  through private for-profit hospitals  that are serving not just wealthy  Nigerians but a broad spectrum of  patients of different income levels so  one of the most exciting things for us  over the last two years is when we learn  about an organization called the medical  credit Fund with a medical credit fund  does is it partners with private  hospitals and it offers them loans well  it doesn't offer them loans it  guarantees loans for them to invest in  capital equipment to improve their  services so they have provided over 13  1,500 loans they work with local banks  so they have 14 local banking partners  that they work with so the banks provide  the loans the medical credit fund  guarantees the loan so they help  hospitals that don't have a credit  history or collateral get ready to go to  the bank they have dispersed over 30  million dollars over the last five years  any guesses as to what their default  rate is  would you believe 3% it's amazing and  we're really excited because the average  loan size is about $20,000 so it's very  comparable to what we project will be  the cost of the nest package and so  we're partnering with the medical credit  Fund to use some of our resources to  play around with the credit terms  because it might be that if you were  willing to target a 5 percent default  rate you might be able to make twice as  many loans medical credit fund knows  right now that they're turning away a  lot of good credit risks but their  investors are not very risk tolerant and  so I think it's a really exciting  opportunity to see how can technology  developers partner with impact investors  to help get their technologies to scale  so finally I want to talk about the the  data that's needed to get to aggregate  demand on the the public sector side so  here what we're doing is we're  partnering with our colleagues at the  London School of Hygiene and Tropical  Medicine and we're beginning to roll out  nest in Malawi we've started with the 8  technologies that are commercially  available and in Malawi what we're doing  is we're evaluating what are the things  that are barriers to scale what are the  conditions that are necessary to get to  scale and using that to develop a theory  of change we will then move to Tanzania  where it will implement a step wedge  trial in southern Tanzania where the  newborn mortality rate is over 40 and  we'll look at what is the impact what  are the cost and what is the  cost-effectiveness of rolling out this  package of technologies to support  comprehensive newborn care and then  we'll work in Nigeria to evaluate this  market-based model of financing  technologies for private sector  facilities we're working as part of the  quality of care network and I think this  is really an amazing opportunity because  these are countries that are committed  to the same goal  we are which is to reduce the deaths of  newborns and hospital facilities 2030 is  only 12 years away we know that you  can't get to 12 without having  technology available no country has been  able to do that and so if we're going to  do it  people need these things yesterday if  they're going to have a chance to make  an impact I think without access to  technologies that make comprehensive  newborn care available we're stuck in  the situation where it will be over a  hundred years before babies in Africa  catch up to all of the benefits that  babies who are born in high-income  countries have benefited from and over a  million babies will continue to die  really unnecessarily in Africa alone so  we think by making this package  available we have the potential to  reduce by half death rates in the next  decade and do it at a cost that has been  acceptable for scaling up vaccines which  have had enormous impact on reducing  childhood death I am part of a much  larger team that includes my colleagues  in Malawi and my colleagues in Tanzania  in Nigeria in Chicago and in London and  it's just been such a pleasure to work  with this amazing group of people and I  want to end with this picture which is  probably my favorite picture in the  whole world this is chicken jutsu and  I've used the little baby that you saw  in the video at the beginning who was  struggling to breathe and this picture  was taken he was just over two years old  when this picture was taken his name  means the Conqueror and our hope is to  enable hundreds of thousands of babies  like chicken jet so to conquer the  challenges that they face right now and  just surviving the first day of life the  first week of life and  make many many more photos like this  possible let me stop there I'm happy to  take any questions thank you  [Applause]  your phones on each side if anybody has  a question please raise your hand thank  you very much oh sorry thanks for  sharing all that wonderful work I wonder  if you could talk a little bit more  specifically about how these innovations  have worked in practice the development  of the innovations have worked in  practice so what are the roles of  students faculty is this around classes  or how have you put it in practice yeah  so what we what we do now is we do a lot  of shadowing of clinicians nurses  clinical officers physicians and really  try and understand what are the barriers  they face and delivering care and then  turn those into design challenges that  will bring two teams of students  sometimes they'll come up with something  that looks promising other times it's  just a good learning opportunity for the  students where they come up with a  prototype that looks promising we will  work with students who are interested in  continuing it as part of an independent  study where that makes sense where we'll  maybe move it up from a freshman to a  capstone design team we have really made  use of post baccalaureate fellowships to  take things from a promising prototype  to something that you can actually get  into clinical evaluation so these are  typically students who are coming in  recent with a recent bachelor's degree  or master's degree and who will spend  usually two years really focusing on  moving that forward sometimes we'll move  some of the projects up out to where a  PhD student is taking it forward so the  Billy Rubin work that I presented  actually started with a sophomore design  team moved into a capstone design team  and then there was a PhD student that  really took it across the finish line  the syringe pump was an example where it  went through to senior design teams  we really sort of got it right and then  there was a post-baccalaureate fellow  who's taken it to where it is now and so  we've got this sort of continuum of  students and staff who are engaged at  various points along the way there's a  very close communication between our  clinical collaborators so we do regular  design reviews to make sure that you  know we don't fall in love with our  technology and end up developing  something that they don't really want  like on the the temperature monitor what  I told the students was don't waste your  money on a digital display you know I  just want a blue light a green light and  a red light and what the nurses said was  whoa we app we absolutely want that  digital display because every four hours  we have to record the temperature and it  will save us so much time if you include  that and at first like I didn't believe  the students so I had asked the nurses  and it was definitely where the feedback  was coming from so you know we tried a  really a guard against biases like that  so they don't and loadshedding is a huge  challenge in this environment the  approach that we're taking is to look at  providing solar backup power together  with the suite of instruments because  it's really difficult to power some of  the devices with battery backup like the  oxygen concentrators for example and the  radiant warmers are the two things that  are the most power hungry  we've tried wherever possible to include  battery backup so the syringe pump has  it the warming current that we've  developed was specially designed to  retain heat but we're exploring solar  backup as a way to power things like the  concentrator and the radiant warmer  counties or maybe cities in this country  that would benefit from implementation  of some of these devices so I think I  think that answer is really device  specific so I think potentially the  Billy Reuben device for example we've  had pediatricians here express a lot of  interest and having access to something  like that we've had interest in the CPAP  as a transport device so for a baby  that's getting transferred from a  community setting to a higher level of  care right now they're providing support  by hand and so this is something that  could easily be used for transport you  know I think when we're designing them  we really think about what is the right  set of features to meet the needs of our  target hospitals and we're we're not so  much taking the approach of can we come  up with something that can work in both  types of settings so it's when there are  opportunities we're really interested in  exploring them thank you for the talk um  some of these technologies seem like  they utilize technologies that are not  so exotic and I'm wondering what the  reason for current devices being so  expensive or what are the primary  reasons that current devices are so  expensive that are unaffordable while 