ich were  killing the electronics and that turned  out to be the case and maybe 15 20  percent of the cases but the much more  common case was that dust in the  environment was going through the case  filter so the case filter is just really  inappropriate for dusty environments we  did in a little experiment where we took  the case filters out we swept up dust in  the NICU and we looked at what fraction  of the dust was trapped by the filter  and it was less than 1% not very  effective that dust gets in the  compressors it causes the seals to fail  as the compressors start to fail then  the SIP beds start to fail and so  they're replacing the cup seals and the  compressors and then they're  regenerating the SIP as I'm using some  really innovative approaches like - that  could give you a small token of our  appreciation you'll find a few yellow  gifts in there and a ceremonial envelope  that reminds you that the honorarium  which is provided by the silliman  endowment of five thousand dollars will  be forthcoming as soon as you return  from africa she is leaving actually  tomorrow evening after she finishes her  visit here with us to head to africa in  closing if i may i'd like to thank a few  people first Joe Howard who is the chair  of the silliman lecture selection  committee and and the remaining members  of the committee Mitzi Campbell from  biomedical engineering  Steve derringer and Bill we're from the  School of Engineering Applied Science  who did most of the work in organizing  this event and I'd like to thank the  student chapter of our biomedical  engineering society as well as our  graduate students for participating and  helping and finally I'd like to thank  everyone for coming and I would also  like to invite you if you will tomorrow  morning at 10 a.m. as part of the  tradition of the silliman lecture it's  really lectures and tomorrow at 10 a.m.  professor Richards quartum will give us  a seminar in biomedical engineering that  will dress some of 